  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nA east-west oriented boundary will be sag slowly south through the \nafternoon and continue overnight. In the moderately unstable airmass\nshowers and storms will continue to develop near this boundary with \nareas along and north of I-70 having the highest probability of \nbeing affected. \n\nThere is potential for locally heavy rainfall due to the moist\nenvironment with PWATs 1.6 to 1.8 inches. Also, slow movement with\nbackbuilding may lead to training storms. Expect some of this\nconvective activity to push south into the overnight hours and\ndiminish. In addition, there is a signal in some of the solutions for\na weakening MCS to move into the area from the west late tonight \nwith the potential for heavy rain impacts greatest across Eastern \nIndiana, Southwest Ohio and North Central KY thru sunrise Saturday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nMid level shortwave to drop south with flow becoming northerly\nSaturday night. Slow moving east-west boundary across Southern Ohio \nwill continue to sag slowly south through the day. Most model \nsolutions continue to show this front pushing south of the area by \nSaturday evening. Showers and thunderstorms will increase in coverage\nand intensity with diurnal heating and the approach of a short wave \nand surface low. PWATs will be 1.8 to 2.0 inches and the warm cloud \ndepth will still be favorable for efficient rainfall rates that can \nlead to locally heavy rainfall. This heavy rain and flood threat will\nlikely linger into Saturday evening along and south of the Ohio \nRiver. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3)\nA large mid level ridge centered over the northern Plains will \nextend eastward into the region for the early to mid part of next \nweek. This will bring generally drier conditions and warmer \ntemperatures. Highs will push into the upper 80s to lower 90s. \nWednesday may be the warmest day with a few locations topping out in\nthe mid 90s. Some uncertainty exists regarding thunderstorm chances \nwith the ECMWF supporting drier conditions and warmer temperatures \nWednesday and the GFS slightly tempering highs and leaning toward a\nchance for thunderstorms.",
